WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:02.970
- [Instructor] We are asked
which expression can we use

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:05.700
to find the missing number in the pattern?

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:08.210
So pause this video and
see if you can try this

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.330
before we do this together.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.010
All right, now let's try this together.

00:00:13.010 --> 00:00:15.210
And the way I would tackle
it is I'll try to see,

00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.500
hey, can I figure out the
pattern between these numbers?

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:20.430
And then I could try to
figure out the missing one.

00:00:20.430 --> 00:00:23.200
So to go from 36 to 45,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:27.060
it looks like I'm increasing
by nine, so I add nine.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:31.680
And it looks like to go from
63 to 72, we're adding nine.

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:33.070
It's good to make sure that the pattern

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:34.970
that you think is happening is happening

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:36.920
across all of the numbers
that you're seeing.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:40.330
And to go from 72 to 81, it
looks like you're adding nine.

00:00:40.330 --> 00:00:41.710
So I'm pretty confident

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:46.100
that to go from 45 to this blank
number, I have to add nine.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.450
And we see here indeed,
choice B is 45 plus nine.

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.400
This number here,

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:52.960
you don't even have to
figure out what it is.

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.520
Some of you might realize it's 54.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.870
But this is just going to be
this number plus nine is going

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:00.130
to go in the blank.

00:01:00.130 --> 00:01:02.420
So 45 plus nine.

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:03.813
Let's do another example.

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:07.280
So here's it's a little bit different.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.370
It says if the pattern continues,

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:11.590
what will be the sixth
number in the pattern?

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.500
Pause this video and
try to figure that out.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.900
All right, so first let's
figure out the pattern.

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:20.270
To go from two to six, it
looks like we added four.

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:23.760
Then we are adding four
again to go from six to 10.

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.500
Then we're adding four
again to go from 10 to 14,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.410
so it seems like we're
adding four every time.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.830
And this is the first one,
two, three, four numbers

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:32.680
in the pattern.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.770
So the fifth number in
the pattern is going

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:38.410
to be 14 plus four,
which is going to be 18.

00:01:38.410 --> 00:01:39.900
And then the sixth number

00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:43.040
in the pattern is going to
be 18 plus four, which is 22.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:43.873
And that's what they want.

00:01:43.873 --> 00:01:46.210
They want the sixth number in the pattern.

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:49.110
So 22 is choice D.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.360
Let's do another example.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:55.350
Here we're told the table
below shows the number

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:59.020
of laps Carly ran around
the track each day.

00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:02.010
Which rule describes the pattern shown

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.900
by the number of laps Carly ran?

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:06.640
So like always, pause this video

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:08.253
and see if you can work on this.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.120
All right, it seems like
every time a day goes by,

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:16.640
the number of laps are going up by three.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:19.830
So if you wanted to
figure out the next day,

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:24.240
let's say day six, you would
add three from day five.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:27.803
So the pattern here is to add three.

